## Example

Let $\Theta\subseteq\mathbb R$. For each $\theta\in\Theta$, suppose
$X_t=\theta+Y_t$, where $Y_t\sim N(0,\tau^2)$ with known $\tau^2>0$ and,
for every $t$, every Borel $B\subseteq\mathbb R$, and every
$A\in\mathcal F_{t-1}$,
$P_\theta(\{Y_t\in B\}\cap A)=P_\theta(Y_t\in B)P_\theta(A)$; take
$\mathcal F_t$ to contain $Y_1,\ldots,Y_t$. Let the reported proxy be any
known $\sigma^2\ge\tau^2$, and fix $\rho>0$ and $\alpha\in(0,1)$. The
Gaussian-mixture intervals with proxy $\sigma^2$ are a $(1-\alpha)$
confidence sequence for $\theta$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Assume AC, $\tau^2>0$, $\sigma^2\ge\tau^2$, $\rho>0$,
$\alpha\in(0,1)$, $Y_t\sim N(0,\tau^2)$, and the displayed event-factorization
identity for every $t$, Borel $B$, and $A\in\mathcal F_{t-1}$.

[F1] [[def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws]]: $N(0,\tau^2)$ is the law of an affine scaling of the standard normal law.

[F2] [[lem-gaussian-exponential-mixture-integral]]: for $\rho'>0$, $s\in\mathbb R$, and $v\ge0$, the Gaussian mixture integral equals $\sqrt{\rho'/(v+\rho')}e^{s^2/[2(v+\rho')]}$.

[F3] [[lem-conditioning-a-known-variable-and-an-independent-variable]]: if Borel events of an integrable random variable factor with every event in $\mathcal G$, its conditional expectation given $\mathcal G$ is its unconditional expectation almost surely.

[F4] [[def-conditional-subgaussian-increments]]: conditional sub-Gaussianity requires the conditional mgf bound for every fixed real $\lambda$ at every time.

[F5] [[thm-subgaussian-mixture-confidence-sequence]]: under that conditional proxy at every time and for every $\lambda$, the stated closed intervals cover the true parameter simultaneously with probability at least $1-\alpha$.

[A1] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: AC is used by the normal-law, conditional-expectation, and confidence-sequence suppliers.

## Verification

1.1 Apply [F2] with $\rho'=1/\tau^2$, $v=0$, and $s=\lambda$. Since $N(0,\tau^2)=N(0,1/\rho')$ by [F1], this gives $\mathbb E e^{\lambda Y_t}=e^{\lambda^2\tau^2/2}$, finite for every real $\lambda$. [given, F1, F2, algebra]

2.1 The event-factorization assumption and [F3] give $\mathbb E[e^{\lambda(X_t-\theta)}\mid\mathcal F_{t-1}]=\mathbb E[e^{\lambda Y_t}\mid\mathcal F_{t-1}]=e^{\lambda^2\tau^2/2}\le e^{\lambda^2\sigma^2/2}$ almost surely for each fixed $t$ and $\lambda\in\mathbb R$. Thus [F4] applies. [given, F3, F4, step 1.1]

3.1 The conditional proxy verified in step 2.1 and [A1] meet the hypotheses of [F5]. Therefore the intervals have simultaneous coverage at least $1-\alpha$ under each $P_\theta$. [F5, A1, step 2.1] ∎
